	<Fragment slot="hero-title">WHY FHUGAWZ EXISTS</Fragment>
	<div class="article-origin">
		<p>I do not think Fhugawz really started on the day I decided to call it Fhugawz.</p>
		<p>When I look back, there are too many things that eventually ended up here: bands, songs that never really found a place, design, images, periods when I left music mostly aside, and others when I came back to it with too many ideas at once.</p>
		<p>For years I thought of all of those things as separate.</p>
		<p>Now I am not so sure.</p>

		<h2>The music was there long before the project existed</h2>
		<p>I started getting seriously involved with music around 2005. At the time, my world was much simpler: guitar, bands, songs, and that obsession that appears when you discover something and immediately want to understand how it works.</p>
		<p>I studied classical guitar for a while, played in different projects, and went through that period when almost any opportunity to make music seemed like a good idea.</p>
		<p>There were cover bands playing bars, experimental projects, metalcore, pop rock, and songs that simply appeared without me really knowing what I was supposed to do with them.</p>
		<p>Two projects became especially important to me: Last Daybreak and Onyx.</p>
		<p>Not because there is a direct line between those bands and what I am doing now, but because that is where I started understanding things that still appear in Fhugawz: the importance of building an identity, defending your own songs, spending a long time working on something even when nobody is waiting for you to make it, and realizing how much a song changes once it stops existing only inside your head.</p>
		<p>I also learned something less romantic: a lot of ideas simply get left behind.</p>
		<p>Sometimes a project ends, people change, life moves somewhere else, or you simply do not yet have the tools to take an idea where you imagined it could go.</p>
		<p>I have quite a few of those ideas.</p>
		<p>Fhugawz probably exists partly because of that.</p>

		<div class="article-image">
			<img src="/images/blog/why-fhugawz-exists/why-fhugawz-exists-design-branding-process.webp" alt="An early band performance from the years before Fhugawz became a defined project." loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
		</div>

		<h2>Then design appeared</h2>
		<p>Around 2015 I started teaching myself design, and eventually it became my work.</p>
		<p>I think that changed the way I looked at music quite a lot, although it took me years to realize it.</p>
		<p>Before that, I could listen to an album and focus almost entirely on the songs. Later I started paying much more attention to how a project was presented, what photographs it used, which typefaces appeared on a cover, how a video was constructed, and why certain artists seemed to inhabit a place of their own even when their sound changed.</p>
		<p>The music was still the center, but I could no longer completely separate what I heard from what I saw.</p>
		<p>Over time I kept learning about design, branding, product, visual direction and a lot of things that, at first, seemed to have nothing directly to do with making records.</p>
		<p>Now it feels fairly obvious that they did.</p>
		<p>A lot of what I am doing with Fhugawz comes precisely from mixing disciplines that I spent years treating as separate.</p>
		<p>I do not have much interest in separating them again.</p>

		<h2>There was a point when everything stopped feeling so abstract</h2>
		<p>There is also a part of the project that comes from a much more complicated period of my life.</p>
		<p>I do not need to reconstruct every detail here. Some of those things already appear in Axioms, and they will probably continue showing up in transformed ways across other records.</p>
		<p>What matters is that after a certain point, returning to music no longer meant exactly the same thing.</p>
		<p>There were many things I did not know how to explain directly, and I started to understand that maybe I did not need to explain them that way.</p>
		<p>They could become songs, images, characters, places or situations that never literally happened and still preserve something true about where they came from.</p>
		<p>That interested me much more.</p>
		<p>Maybe that is why the project began in memory and gradually started moving away from it.</p>
		<p>At first there are fairly recognizable memories. Later those memories begin to distort, mix with fiction, horror, technology, surrealism and things that no longer clearly belong to any specific experience.</p>
		<p>At some point I stopped wanting only to tell what had happened.</p>
		<p>I started wondering what I could build from it.</p>

		<h2>The references started mixing too</h2>
		<p>I have never had one single reference for Fhugawz.</p>
		<p>I can recognize things that come from Nirvana, Secret &amp; Whisper, Pierce the Veil, Sullii, Billie Eilish and many other artists who probably would not seem to belong on the same list.</p>
		<p>But that is exactly what I like about it.</p>
		<p>From some I am drawn to the rawness, from others the atmosphere, fragility, the way a voice is produced, emotional intensity, or the ability to build a visual identity that already exists before the first song even starts.</p>
		<p>I am not trying to create a mathematical combination of all those references.</p>
		<p>Usually something much less organized happens.</p>
		<p>I listen to something for years, then another song appears that seemingly has nothing to do with it, and I discover that a certain decision came from somewhere that had been sitting with me for a very long time.</p>
		<p>That is also why I do not want Fhugawz to always sound the same.</p>
		<p>It would actually feel strange if it did.</p>

		<h2>I think that is where the Worlds appeared</h2>
		<p>For a long time I looked for coherence by trying to find one consistent sound.</p>
		<p>Now I think I was looking in the wrong place.</p>
		<p>The coherence of Fhugawz is not really in genre.</p>
		<p>It is in continuity.</p>
		<p>The thirteen Worlds can move between folk, post-hardcore, dark pop, glitch, shoegaze, grunge, horror, rock or things I still do not know exactly how to classify. The images can change dramatically from one record to another too.</p>
		<p>What I am trying to preserve is something else: the feeling that all of them belong to the same evolution.</p>
		<p>Each World represents a different state and occupies a specific place inside the sequence. What happens in one leaves something prepared for the next, even when musically they seem very far apart.</p>
		<p>That solved a problem I had been carrying for years.</p>
		<p>I no longer need to force all of my influences into the same record.</p>
		<p>They can transform with the story.</p>

		<div class="article-image">
			<img src="/images/blog/why-fhugawz-exists/why-fhugawz-exists-dark-pop-creative-world.webp.webp" alt="A nocturnal personal image connected to the evolving Fhugawz creative world." loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
		</div>

		<h2>I am also understanding the project while I build it</h2>
		<p>One important thing is that a lot of this was not planned from the beginning.</p>
		<p>It would be very easy to write about it now as if I had always had a perfectly organized map of thirteen albums, a complete narrative and a fully defined visual direction.</p>
		<p>That was not the case.</p>
		<p>A lot of things appeared while working.</p>
		<p>Others changed several times.</p>
		<p>Some ideas that seemed fundamental eventually disappeared, while details that initially felt minor became much more important than I expected.</p>
		<p>That still happens.</p>
		<p>There are Worlds whose identity I understand fairly well and others I am still trying to figure out. Some songs will probably change dramatically once I rebuild them. There are parts of the narrative that work today and maybe a year from now I will find a better way to connect them.</p>
		<p>That does not bother me too much.</p>
		<p>In fact, one of the reasons I want to keep this Journal is to record those transformations too.</p>
		<p>Not only the result.</p>

		<h2>So why does Fhugawz exist?</h2>
		<p>I suppose it exists because for too long I had music on one side, design somewhere else, stories in another place, and a lot of ideas that did not seem to belong anywhere.</p>
		<p>Fhugawz became the place where I can finally let all of those things meet.</p>
		<p>I do not need every song to explain the entire project or every album to use the same language. I only need to be able to step far enough away and still recognize that they are parts of the same thing.</p>
		<p>That is what I am trying to build now.</p>
		<p>Thirteen Worlds, different stages, sounds that change, images that change, and a story that is slowly beginning to reveal why they are connected.</p>
		<p>There is still a lot that is unfinished.</p>
		<p>Maybe that is exactly why it makes sense to write about it now.</p>
	</div>
</ArticleLayout>
